This directory contains only the embedded engine source needed by the private
neograph_quickjs target: quickjs.c, cutils.c, dtoa.c, libregexp.c,
libunicode.c, and their transitive headers. It intentionally excludes
quickjs-libc.c, quickjs-libc.h, qjs.c, qjsc.c, repl.js, and all standalone
examples/tests. NeoGraph therefore neither registers QuickJS std/os modules
nor supplies a dynamic native-module loader.

QuickJS bytecode is never a durable NeoGraph artifact. Durable JavaScript
identity is source plus the pinned release, archive digest, and NeoGraph build
configuration.

Local patches (keep when updating the vendored release)

- quickjs.c js_realloc_array: the growth (*psize * 3 / 2) and the byte size
  (new_size * elem_size) are computed in 64 bits, and a size that does not fit
  an int element count or size_t bytes throws OutOfMemory. Upstream marks this
  spot "XXX: potential arithmetic overflow"; the int multiplication could wrap
  and allocate less than the size recorded in *psize. Found by CodeQL
  (cpp/integer-multiplication-cast-to-long).
- quickjs.c stack limit on Emscripten. Upstream defines CONFIG_STACK_CHECK
  only when __EMSCRIPTEN__ is not defined, so JS_SetMaxStackSize had no effect
  in a WebAssembly build: a source that recursed without end exhausted the
  host's call stack, which the host reports as an uncatchable RangeError that
  leaves the module instance unusable. The patch defines CONFIG_STACK_CHECK on
  every target, so js_check_stack_overflow compares __builtin_frame_address(0),
  which on WebAssembly is the pointer of the shadow stack in linear memory,
  with rt->stack_limit. On Emscripten only: update_stack_limit() honours the
  size as far as the shadow stack holds, keeps the limit
  NEOGRAPH_QUICKJS_WASM_STACK_RESERVE (16 KiB) above emscripten_stack_get_end(),
  and reads a size of 0, which upstream takes for "no limit", as all the shadow
  stack allows; JS_GetAppliedMaxStackSize(rt) (declared in neograph/quickjs.h,
  renamed in neograph/quickjs-prefix.h) returns the size in force, so that the
  witness of a limit diagnostic reports it. js_call_bound_function allocates
  NEOGRAPH_QUICKJS_WASM_BOUND_FRAME_PAD (128) bytes more per level. The size is
  the embedder's choice: NeoGraph's Program runtime asks for 48 KiB unless its
  host sets another (JavaScriptCompileLimits::kDefaultStackBytes). The host's
  stack (about 984 KiB under V8, Node 22 and 24) is used at 1.5 to 7 times the
  rate of the shadow stack, depending on the recursion (measured with the 17
  shapes of wasm/test/program_test.mjs): JavaScript calls at the low end (1.5 to
  1.7), the parser and Array.prototype.flat at 4 to 6 times, JSON.stringify of
  nested data at 6 to 7 times. A chain of bound functions used it 20 times as
  fast until the pad. All but two of the 84 recursion shapes that were tried (calls, getters, Proxy traps,
  callbacks of builtins, generators, nested source, JSON, flat) are caught at
  48 KiB, and are still caught with the size doubled, on both Node versions with
  and without the baseline compiler (wasm/README.md has the sizes that
  wasm/test/program_test.mjs's shapes tolerate). A size that the host's stack
  cannot carry ends in a trap, which the module cannot recover from. The two
  that no size catches are Object.getPrototypeOf and Object.isExtensible
  through a chain of about 5,000 Proxy objects without traps, which upstream
  does not check on any target. The text of
  js_get_stack_pointer is unchanged, and so are the needles of
  cmake/NeoGraphQuickJSMsvcPort.cmake. Other targets are unchanged.
- quickjs.c build_backtrace holds a reference to the error object while it
  records the backtrace. Its callers pass rt->current_exception, and an
  allocation inside it that runs out of memory raises a new exception, which
  frees the previous one; the function then defined properties on the freed
  object. A script that ran out of memory at that point could crash the
  process (seen on Windows as an access violation in the memory-limit test of
  the Program gate) instead of failing with the out-of-memory error. The two
  early returns now release the reference and the dynamic buffer too, and a
  stack text that cannot be allocated is stored as null rather than as the
  exception marker. Upstream
  issue https://github.com/bellard/quickjs/issues/533; the patch has the shape
  of the open upstream fix https://github.com/bellard/quickjs/pull/565, so a
  release that merges it replaces this patch. The needles of
  cmake/NeoGraphQuickJSMsvcPort.cmake are unchanged.

